/* ============================================================================
extra-payment-savings.epher -- how an extra 100 a month shortens a 30-year
mortgage and cuts its interest

The borrower pays the regular monthly payment plus a fixed extra amount.
Each month the balance grows by one month of interest and then loses the
boosted payment; the packed while loop below counts the payments until
the balance turns zero or negative. The baseline is the same loan at its
minimum payment over the full 360 months. Source: the amortization
recurrence, Kellison, The Theory of Interest, chapter 3.

Accuracy: 12 displayed digits. The loop's last full payment overshoots,
so the true final payment is smaller; the interest figures below account
for that smaller payment.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/finance/loans/extra-payment-savings.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\finance\loans\extra-payment-savings.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/finance/loans/extra-payment-savings.epher

The knobs to edit are in the knob section; everything after it is
machinery you can leave alone.
============================================================================ */

// ---- knobs ------------------------------------------------------------------
const principal = 200000    // loan amount at closing, USD
const rate = 6%             // quoted annual rate
const years = 30            // term in years
const extra = 100           // extra paid every month on top of the minimum, USD
const payment = tvm_pmt(years * 12, rate / 12, -principal, 0)   // minimum monthly payment, USD
const boosted = payment + extra                                  // payment with the extra 100

/* ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
The baseline: 360 payments at the minimum, then the packed loop for the
boosted payment. The loop state is {payments made, balance}; it stops as
soon as a payment leaves the balance at zero or below. */
v = {0, principal}
while v[2] > 0 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] * (1 + rate / 12) - boosted}
print("minimum payment:", payment)
print("months to pay off with the extra 100:", v[1])
print("true last payment (smaller):", boosted + v[2])
print("interest paid with the extra 100:", v[1] * boosted + v[2] - principal)
print("interest paid on the baseline plan:", years * 12 * payment - principal)
print("interest saved:", (years * 12 * payment - principal) - (v[1] * boosted + v[2] - principal))
/* The extra 100 cuts the term from 360 months to 295 (24.6 years) and saves
about 49138.41 of interest. The builtin tvm_n gives the fractional term
294.46 months on the same numbers, consistent with 295 full payments and
a smaller last one. */
/* ---- expected output ----
= 200000
= 0.06
= 30
= 100
= 1199.10105031
= 1299.10105031
= {0, 200000}
= minimum payment: 1199.10105031
= months to pay off with the extra 100: 295
= true last payment (smaller): 602.261007733
= interest paid with the extra 100: 182537.969798
= interest paid on the baseline plan: 231676.37811
= interest saved: 49138.4083124
*/
